-- std/secret - Gated secret resolution (M-SECRET-EFFECT, v0.26.0)
--
-- Resolve a secret reference to its value behind approval. WARNING: the <secret> label does NOT keep the value out of execution traces -- do not enable tracing when resolving secrets (see below).
--
-- Resolution is gated behind a (possibly remote, human) approval. The
-- reference is safe to pass around and
-- log; the resolved value is labelled <secret> (M-TAINT-TYPES) so the type
-- system forbids it from reaching a {not secret} SINK -- a function whose
-- signature demands it, such as a network body -- without an explicit
-- ! {Declassify} step.
--
-- !! EXECUTION TRACES ARE NOT COVERED BY THAT GUARANTEE (as of v0.35.4).
--
-- An earlier version of this comment listed "logs, traces" among the protected
-- sinks. That was wrong, and it was wrong in the dangerous direction: it told a
-- security-conscious team they were protected when they were not.
--
-- Tracing runs BELOW the type system. `-emit-trace` renders every function's
-- arguments and results with String() before the trace subsystem sees them, so
-- the label -- which is erased before runtime -- cannot reach it. A <secret>
-- value crossing any traced call boundary is written to the trace VERBATIM.
-- Measured: a value a program prints 6 characters of appears in full 4 times.
--
-- Until this is fixed, if you resolve a secret, do not enable tracing:
--   AILANG_TRACE=off   (or simply do not pass -emit-trace)
--
-- Tracked as M-TRACE-LABEL-AWARE, reported as fb_5c0baeb1ee5ae1eb.
-- See design_docs/planned/v0_36_0/m-trace-label-aware.md
--
-- Trust model A: resolution runs on the server via the 1Password CLI with a
-- service-account token. Approval is pushed to the operator's device.
--
-- Requires: Secret capability (--caps Secret)

module std/secret

-- secret: Resolve an op:// reference to its plaintext value after approval. WARNING: the <secret> label does not cover execution traces; a traced call boundary writes this value verbatim (M-TRACE-LABEL-AWARE).
-- The result is labelled <secret> (M-TAINT-TYPES): CheckModuleIFC forbids it
-- from reaching a {not secret} sink without an explicit ! {Declassify} step.
-- That covers sinks the type system can see. It does NOT cover execution
-- traces -- see the warning in the module header.
-- `purpose` is the human-readable reason shown in the approval request.
-- Example: let key = secret("op://Prod/stripe/api-key", "charge order #1234")
export func secret(ref: string, purpose: string) -> string<secret> ! {Secret} = _secret_read(ref, purpose)
